course: everyday
lesson: 513
title: Grandparents abroad and grandparents you never met
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Every Sunday, Taras in Leeds video calls his baba and dido (Ukrainian for gran and grandad) in Ukraine. Baba walks the phone round her garden to show him the tomatoes, then says quietly that the air-raid sirens were loud last night. Taras doesn't know what to say. Lots of children have grandparents far away, some in countries at war. Hearing about danger is hard, and it helps to tell Mum or Dad how it feels. And the calls matter hugely to Baba: seeing his face is the best part of her week.
explain: Language gaps are common, and they can be funny. In Vancouver, Lin's Mandarin is «mid» (Gen Z slang for just average). She tries to tell Nainai (grandma, on her dad's side) «I love you», gets a tone wrong so the word means something else, and Nainai laughs and laughs. In Auckland, Koa's grandad in Samoa sends voice messages in Samoan; Koa understands half, and his mum translates the rest. In Toronto, Sophie-Grace's gran rings from Scotland: «Ye're lookin' braw, hen!» «Huh?» («braw» is lovely, «hen» is dear.) Trying matters more than getting it right.
explain: Love travels in parcels. Every Diwali (the Hindu festival of lights), Nani (grandma, on mum's side) sends Pooja in Leicester a box of sweets from Delhi, and Pooja sends drawings back. In Sydney, Ethan-Jay's grandparents post Cadbury chocolate and Marmite from England; his mates go, «Ew, Marmite?», and he eats it anyway. In Dublin, Babcia (Polish for grandma) sends Olga and her mam a recipe for pierogi (dumplings); theirs come out like soggy slippers, and Babcia laughs on video till she cries. «Again next week!»
explain: In Birmingham, Zara-Leigh's grandma rings from Jamaica: «Wah gwaan, mi baby?» (Jamaican Patois for what's going on, my baby?). «Mi deh yah, Grandma» (I'm here, I'm fine). Ayo, 10, from London, visits Lagos once and can't believe how many people know his name: «You're Mama Bisi's grandson!» Visiting your grandparents' country can make you feel part of something big.
explain: Some grandparents you never meet. In Belfast, Jago's mates talk about their grannies, and Jago says, «I never met mine.» They died before he was born. He looks at old photos with his dad: Granda in a flat cap, Granny laughing at a wedding. You can still know grandparents you never met, through photos, stories and the things they left behind. Just ask.
fact: Many children have grandparents in other countries, and video calls, messages and parcels keep them close.
fact: Hearing about danger, like a war where a grandparent lives, is hard; telling a parent how you feel helps.
fact: Calls from grandchildren mean a lot to grandparents far away.
fact: In Mandarin, the tone of a word changes its meaning, so a wrong tone can make a funny mistake.
fact: Language gaps with grandparents are common; trying matters more than getting it right.
fact: Learning a few words of a grandparent's language makes them very happy.
fact: Cooking a grandparent's recipe keeps family traditions alive, even when it goes wrong at first.
fact: Different countries have different tastes; one family's treat, like Marmite, is another's «ew».
fact: Visiting a grandparent's country can connect a child to their roots.
fact: Some children never meet their grandparents; photos and stories can still help them know them.
fact: Gran in Ukrainian is «baba», grandad is «dido»; in Mandarin a father's mother is «nainai»; in Hindi a mother's mother is «nani»; in Polish grandma is «babcia».
fact: «Mid» is Gen Z slang for average; «braw» is Scots for lovely and «hen» for dear; «Wah gwaan» is Jamaican Patois for what's going on; pierogi are Polish dumplings; «rank» is UK slang for disgusting.
exercise: What did Baba say on the Sunday call that Taras didn't know how to answer? | The sirens were loud last night | The tomatoes in her garden were growing nicely | She was moving to Leeds | She had forgotten his birthday this year
exercise: What can help Taras when calls with Baba make him scared? | Telling his parents how he feels | Stopping the Sunday calls for good | Pretending he never heard about it | Keeping it all to himself
exercise: Why did Nainai laugh at Lin's «I love you»? | She got a tone wrong | She said it in English | She said it too loudly | She said it to the cat by mistake
exercise: What does «mid» mean in Gen Z slang? | Average | Great | Middle child | Midnight
exercise: How does Koa understand his grandad's Samoan voice messages? | His mum translates the parts he doesn't get | He uses a translating app on his tablet | He doesn't, he just deletes them all | His grandad sends them again in English
exercise: Gran says to Sophie-Grace, «Ye're lookin' braw, hen». What does she mean? | You're looking lovely, dear | You're looking like a chicken | You look tired and pale, dear | You've grown!
exercise: What does Pooja send back to Nani for Diwali? | Drawings | Sweets from Leicester | Money for the post | Nothing
exercise: What happened when Olga and her mam cooked Babcia's pierogi? | They were a disaster and Babcia laughed | They were perfect at the very first go | Babcia was angry that they got it wrong | They gave up and bought pizza instead
exercise: What are pierogi? | Polish dumplings | Polish pancakes | A Polish cake with plums | Polish sausages
exercise: Ethan-Jay's mates say «Ew, Marmite?». What does he do? | Eats it anyway, he loves it | Throws it in the bin | Sends it back to England | Says he hates it too
exercise: Grandma says, «Wah gwaan, mi baby?». What is she asking? | What's going on, my baby? | Where are you going, my baby? | Why are you crying, my baby? | When are you coming, my baby?
exercise: What surprised Ayo in Lagos? | So many people knew his name | Nobody spoke English | It was cold and snowing when he landed | His grandma didn't recognise him
exercise: Why did Jago never meet his grandparents? | They died before he was even born | They live on the other side of the world | They had fallen out with his dad | They were too busy to visit him
exercise: How can Jago get to know grandparents he never met? | Through old photos and family stories | He can't, it's far too late to know anything | Only by visiting their graves every day | By making up his own stories about them
test: Lin in Vancouver calls at 6 in the evening on Saturday. Chengdu is 15 hours ahead (in summer). What time is it for Nainai? | 9 in the morning on Sunday | 9 in the evening on Saturday | 3 in the morning on Saturday | 6 in the morning on Sunday
test: Taras's Baba is 2 hours ahead of Leeds. Taras calls at 10:30 Sunday morning, Leeds time. What time is it for Baba? | 12:30 | 8:30 | 10:30 | 2:30
test: Lin: «my Mandarin is so mid, I'm never trying again lol». What's the better thought? | Trying matters more than getting it right | Nainai will be cross with every mistake | Only perfect speakers should ever try | Mandarin can't be learned outside China
test: Taras: «Baba says there were sirens… I don't wanna call any more, it's too sad». What would most likely happen if he stopped calling? | Baba would miss him badly | Baba would be safer straight away | Nothing, she wouldn't notice | The sirens would stop for good
test: Ethan-Jay's mate: «Marmite's rank, mate, your family's weird». What's the truth? | Different countries have different tastes | Marmite is poisonous for kids | His family is weird for liking it | All English people hate Marmite
test: A cartoon shows grandparents abroad as sad people waiting by the phone all day. What's truer? | They have busy lives and love the calls | They do nothing but wait by the phone | They don't care about their grandchildren | They can't use phones at all
test: Babcia: «Again next week!» after the pierogi turned out like soggy slippers. What's the point of trying again? | Keeping the family recipe alive | Winning a cooking competition | Making Babcia stop laughing | Using up all the flour in the house
test: Jago: «I never met my granny, so she's not really my granny». Right? | No, she's still his granny, and he can learn about her | Yes, you have to meet someone for them to count | Yes, only living grandparents are real family | No, but he should never ask about her
test: Zara-Leigh: «Mi deh yah, Grandma». What is she saying? | I'm here, I'm fine | I'm hungry, Grandma | I'm going out now | I miss your cooking
test: Koa wants to understand his grandad without Mum translating. What's the best plan? | Learn some Samoan words | Ask Grandad to stop sending messages | Only listen to English songs | Wait for Grandad to learn English
test: Pooja, Nani's parcel and Pooja's drawings travel between Leicester and Delhi. Which of these is NOT a way they keep close? | Never writing, so Nani doesn't worry | Sending sweets at Diwali | Sending drawings back | Calling and talking
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the significance of regular communication between a child and a grandparent resident abroad? | It is of great value to the grandparent | It is of no consequence to either party | It is permissible solely upon public holidays | It is detrimental to the grandparent's wellbeing
exam: Identify the principal reason why a single mispronounced tone may alter the meaning of a word in Mandarin. | Tone is part of a word's meaning in that language | Mandarin words possess no fixed meaning of any kind | Mandarin is spoken without any variation in pitch | Only the first syllable of each word carries meaning
exam: Ascertain the most appropriate means by which a child may acquire knowledge of grandparents deceased before the child's birth. | Photographs and family recollections | The invention of imaginary biographies by the child | No means exist whereby such knowledge may be acquired | Exclusively the daily attendance of the place of burial
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning a child who hears from a grandparent of danger in a country at war. | Disclosure of the child's feelings to a parent is beneficial | The child ought to conceal all such feelings permanently | The child ought to discontinue all further communication | The child bears responsibility for the grandparent's safety
